[Library "Italian Game"]
[Side "white"]
[Description "A classical, principled 1.e4 e5 repertoire built around quick development and central control."]
[Event "Giuoco Piano: 3...Bc5"]

1. e4 {Claim the center and open lines for the queen and bishop.} e5 2. Nf3 {Develop with tempo against e5.} Nc6 3. Bc4 {The Italian bishop eyes f7, Black's weakest square.} Bc5 4. c3 {Preparing d4 to build a big center.} Nf6 (4... d6 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb6 7. Nc3 {White has the ideal center.}) 5. d3 {The modern, slow "Giuoco Pianissimo" — solid and rich in ideas.} d6 (5... O-O 6. O-O d5 7. exd5 Nxd5 8. Re1 {Pressure on e5.}) 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1 a6 8. Bb3 {Tucking the bishop away from ...Na5 and ...d5 ideas.} Ba7 9. h3 {Stopping ...Bg4 before regrouping with Nbd2-f1-g3.} *

[Library "Italian Game"]
[Side "white"]
[Event "Two Knights: 3...Nf6"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 {The Two Knights Defense — more combative.} 4. d3 {Sidestepping the sharp 4.Ng5 lines and keeping e4 protected.} Be7 (4... Bc5 5. c3 {Transposes to Giuoco Piano structures.} d6 6. O-O O-O) 5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 d6 7. c3 {The classic plan: Nbd2-f1-g3 and a later d4.} a6 8. Bb3 Na5 9. Bc2 {Keep the bishop on the b1-h7 diagonal.} c5 10. Nbd2 *

